/* ==========================================================================
** CodeMirror 6 - themes
**
** This sheet deliberately does almost nothing, and that is the design.
**
** The plugin exists so a user can choose a syntax theme - Nord, Dracula,
** Gruvbox, Solarized and fourteen more. Someone who has picked one has made a
** decision, and a page theme that re-tinted it would be overriding a choice
** rather than styling an unstyled surface. So none of the --wt-* variables the
** themes set are touched here.
**
** The mechanism makes that easy to respect. Each theme declares its variables
** on [data-cm6-theme="name"], and the plugin sets that attribute on the EDITOR
** element (files/editor/widgets/subclasses/edit-text.js). The Ironworks
** defaults in .../plugins/codemirror-6 are on :root. The editor element is
** deeper in the inheritance chain, so a chosen theme wins over the Ironworks
** defaults without either sheet having to know about the other, and Ironworks
** is what you get when no theme is chosen.
**
** What IS worth doing is making sure a chosen theme sits in the page properly.
** A CM6 theme colours the text and the editor's own background; it says
** nothing about the frame around it. Left alone, a dark theme in a light wiki
** is a dark rectangle butted straight against a light ground with a border that
** belongs to neither.
** ========================================================================== */

/*
** The frame follows the theme rather than the page when a theme is active.
** currentColor is the theme's own text colour, so the border is derived from
** whatever theme is loaded without this sheet needing to know any of their
** names - which matters, because the plugin ships eighteen and will ship more.
*/
.tc-editor-codemirror6[data-cm6-theme] .cm-editor,
.tc-editor-codemirror6 .cm-editor[data-cm6-theme] {
	border-radius: var(--iw-radius);
}

.tc-editor-codemirror6:has([data-cm6-theme]),
.tc-editor-codemirror6[data-cm6-theme] {
	border-color: color-mix(in srgb, currentColor 22%, transparent);
	/* The theme owns the inside of the editor, so the wrapper stops painting a
	   background of its own behind it - otherwise the theme's colour sits on a
	   sliver of the theme's plate at the edges. */
	background: transparent;
}


/*
** A CM6 syntax theme replaces the editor's background with its own, and the
** ruling is mixed against the editor surface this theme supplies — so under a
** syntax theme those lines are drawn for a surface that is no longer there: on
** a dark theme in a light wiki they vanish, on a light theme in a dark wiki
** they turn into hard bright stripes across the code.
**
** Drafting stock is an Ironworks idea about writing prose. A syntax theme is a
** deliberate choice to make the editor look like something else, and the two
** should not be layered.
*/
.iw-scribed-editor .tc-editor-codemirror6:has([data-cm6-theme]) .cm-content,
.iw-scribed-editor .tc-editor-codemirror6[data-cm6-theme] .cm-content {
	background-image: none;
}

</$list>
